    scenario: 'The current kiln decision starts at setpoint 880°C: H holds it, while U has a=0.5 and proposes a 1°C increase over 5 minutes. To put every probability on paper, separately define a fictional two-state MDP: S means stable and W disturbed. It does not track cumulative absolute setpoints and is not a physical kiln model. The full system still needs history, soft measurements, released assays, gates and separate costs.',
    decisionQuestion: 'If infinitely many intervals remain, why does “one reward plus next-state value” still include the entire future?',
    intuition: 'Cut a long account after its first entry. The remainder is another account with the same structure. A Bellman equation performs that algebraic split, then averages over actions and next states. The difficult part is usually stating the conditions, probabilities and quantities being averaged clearly.',
