        explanation: 'a′ enumerates next-state candidates; it is not the particular sampled next action. The maximum picks H’s 4. Even when the recorded behavior continues with U, Q-learning uses a greedy tail, separating behavior and target policies.',
        example: 'The same current entry 2 with α=0.1 becomes 2+0.1×(4.6−2)=2.26. The difference from SARSA’s 1.99 is not an arithmetic error; they evaluate different continuation rules.',
        why: 'Classical tabular Q-learning results require conditions on coverage, stepsizes and finite environments; see [Watkins and Dayan](https://www.gatsby.ucl.ac.uk/~dayan/papers/wd92.html). They do not transfer without qualification to arbitrary neural networks or fixed logs.',
